{"builder": {"source": "x", "name": "Guillermo Rauch", "handle": "rauchg", "bio": "@vercel CEO", "tweets": [{"id": "2107962327169675566", "text": "One thing the engineering community will (re)discover is that every program can be ① hardened (edge cases squashed, inputs tightened, errors handled) and ② optimized (profiled, benchmarked, rewritten) basically ad infinitum.

There are real costs in every direction: time, attention, opportunity. You can literally explore input spaces that will never be hit or optimize performance of something that will never be used ("you have no users".) Even in an infinite token world, you need to know when to stop and what tradeoffs to accept. Agents will happily drill no matter what.", "createdAt": "2026-10-07T22:32:37.000Z", "url": "https://x.com/rauchg/status/2107962327169675566", "likes": 605, "retweets": 39, "replies": 100, "isQuote": false, "quotedTweetId": null}, {"id": "2107861196506595398", "text": "gm from the best city in the world https://t.co/aPDFNR7KhT", "createdAt": "2026-10-07T15:50:46.000Z", "url": "https://x.com/rauchg/status/2107861196506595398", "likes": 784, "retweets": 11, "replies": 50, "isQuote": false, "quotedTweetId": null}]}, "tweets": [{"id": "2107962327169675566", "text": "One thing the engineering community will (re)discover is that every program can be ① hardened (edge cases squashed, inputs tightened, errors handled) and ② optimized (profiled, benchmarked, rewritten) basically ad infinitum.

There are real costs in every direction: time, attention, opportunity. You can literally explore input spaces that will never be hit or optimize performance of something that will never be used ("you have no users".) Even in an infinite token world, you need to know when to stop and what tradeoffs to accept. Agents will happily drill no matter what.", "createdAt": "2026-10-07T22:32:37.000Z", "url": "https://x.com/rauchg/status/2107962327169675566", "likes": 605, "retweets": 39, "replies": 100, "isQuote": false, "quotedTweetId": null}]}

